                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I tested it on a water glass.
   It was one of Mamá’s, from the set of eight she bought at the Path‐
mark in 2009 and has counted every Sunday since. I set it on the desk
next to the pipe, and I tapped the key, and the glass began to sing. Not
loud. It was a thin, high, wrong little note, like a finger on a wet rim,
and the water inside shivered into a pattern of rings.
   Then a hairline crawled up the side.
   Then the glass came apart in a single clean motion, and the water
ran off the desk.
   I sat there with my hands in the air like a man who’d been told to
freeze. And under the embarrassment, under the arithmetic of what I
owed my mother, there was a feeling I’d never had in my life. Some‐
thing had answered me, instantly, completely, with no static in it at all.
I’d spent twenty-five years being a person things happened to. For four
seconds I was the thing that happened.
   It was the best I had ever felt.
   Seven glasses left. I did the math for Mamá, and I did the math for
the block, and I did not do the math on what comes after a feeling like
that.
   I looked out the window.
   The building across the street still wore its blue netting. And on the
third floor, like every night since March, one window was lit.



I didn’t decide. I’m going to tell you that, and you’re going to know it’s
a lie.
    I thought about my mother’s can, and the seven letters in a Ziploc,
and a man with a basket on a mat that said BENDICIONES. I kept see‐
ing three dots that had lasted eight seconds and stopped. I pictured a
man in a window, in headphones, facing our building with his
shoulders up like it was the ninth inning, listening to my life through a
pipe.
    I put my hand on the cold iron and aimed the mirror at the lit glass.
    The window cracked in a long, bright line, corner to corner.




